{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792923601,"nonce":"ajtV7q27AO2uy58ZXT+gTOLuA1A=","title":"LSP: one Haxe face for a module of another language","message":"Part of 1626b90d930d209e848220743e00494408af449d2cdabdddea70f6c0a5337545 (caribou lsp).\n\nToday the build macro (haxe/caribou/Bridge.hx) maps each ModuleDesc that\n`caribou describe` prints to the Haxe classes the program compiles\nagainst. The language server needs the same classes. Two copies of the\nmapping would drift, and an editor that disagrees with the compiler is\nworse than none.\n\n- Move the mapping from a ModuleDesc to Haxe declarations into Rust, in the\n  workspace:\n  - one class per exported class, named by the module's path;\n  - members with their signatures;\n  - enums;\n  - a plugin's classes under its package;\n  - a Zyntax module's published classes.\n- caribou-lsp's provider uses it in process.\n- `caribou describe` prints the same declarations for the macro, which\n  defines them as it does today, so `-lib caribou` stays the only thing a\n  program adds.\n- The interop tests that compile Haxe against Wren, plugin and Zyntax\n  modules keep passing on the moved mapping. A test compares what the\n  provider declares with what the macro defines for the same roots.","files":null}]}